<title>FRANCE: French court orders Renault to call plant meeting.</title>
<headline>French court orders Renault to call plant meeting.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-05-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Court of Appeal in Versailles on Wednesday ruled that car maker Renault SA must call a meeting of its European works council before it can proceed with the closure of the Vilvoorde plant in Belgium.</p>
<p>Renault appealed against a ruling by a Nanterre court which said that the car maker had not followed the proper procedures in arriving at the Vilvoorde closure decision.</p>
<p>The car maker had planned to close Vilvoorde, near Brussels, at the end of July.</p>
<p>The plant employes some 3,100 staff and the February 27 closure announcement sparked wide protest by Belgian car workers.</p>
<p>Renault chairman Louis Schweitzer said in April that a slight delay in the closure was possible in the closure but the decision itself was irrevocable.</p>
<p>Renault said it decided to close Vilvoorde because it needed to slash its cost base and improve competitiveness.</p>
</text>
